.TH OMX\-CLAP\-HOST 1 "October 2026" "omx-clap-host @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
omx\-clap\-host \- CLAP and LV2 plugin host for JACK, driven over mod\-host's socket protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B omx\-clap\-host
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR channels ]
.RB [ \-S ]
.RB [ \-\-thread\-pool =\fImode\fR]
.br
.B omx\-clap\-host
.RB { \-V | \-h }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omx\-clap\-host
loads CLAP and LV2 plugins and runs each instance as a JACK client named
.BI effect_ N
with one port per channel of the plugin's main audio pair,
.BI in_ k
and
.BI out_ k
from 1, exactly as
.BR mod\-host (1)
lays out an LV2 plugin, and one per channel of each auxiliary port,
.BI aux_in_ p _ k
and
.BI aux_out_ p _ k\fR,
.I p
the port's place among the side's auxiliary ports and
.I k
its channel, both from 1. It is controlled over the same socket, with the
same line protocol and the same replies as mod\-host, from mod\-host's
protocol library, so a controller that speaks to mod\-host can speak to
this host unchanged.
.PP
A plugin is admitted with exactly one main audio output and at most one
main audio input, each of up to 64 channels and of any widths, every channel
a JACK port. A plugin with a note input, an
instrument, may have no audio input at all. A plugin with one note input
gets a JACK MIDI input port,
.BR midi_in ,
on its client:
note on and note off arrive as CLAP note events with a note id, or as MIDI
events when the input prefers the MIDI or the MIDI MPE dialect, on the frame
they arrived on. An id is free again when the plugin ends its voice. In an MPE
zone a member channel's bend, pressure and controller 74 become per-note
expressions of the note on that channel. Controllers, program change and
system messages reach only an input that reads MIDI events. A plugin with a
note output gets a JACK MIDI output port,
.BR midi_out :
the notes and MIDI the plugin sends out on its first note output are written
there in the same cycle, at their frame. A port of no channels or of
more than 64, more than 64 auxiliary ports on a side, a second main port on a side, a second note input
or a note input read in none of those dialects is refused at
.B add
with
.B resp \-102
followed by its hosting code, which standard error carries too. An auxiliary
audio input (a sidechain) reads its JACK ports, silence while nothing is
linked to them; an auxiliary audio output writes its JACK ports.
.PP
An LV2 plugin is added by its URI,
.BI lv2: uri
or the URI alone as mod\-host takes it, from the bundle on
.B LV2_PATH
that holds it. It runs natively, a real LV2 instance loaded through lilv,
with no wrapper process; the LV2 adapter linked into the host maps the host
interface, presenting its ports and controls as CLAP ports and parameters, so
it runs on the same ports and under the same rules as a CLAP plugin. A plugin
the adapter cannot map is refused with
.B resp \-102
and the adapter's hosting code, and a URI no bundle holds with
.BR "resp \-101" .
Its control ports are addressed by their LV2 symbols, its output control ports
are output symbols, its
.B lv2:enabled
port is the host's bypass, and
.B preset_load
takes a preset's URI. lilv is loaded on the first LV2 add.
.PP
The plugin's latency is published on its JACK ports and republished when
the plugin changes it. Bypass is the host's own one\-block crossfade to the
dry input, or to silence for an instrument; the plugin's own bypass parameter is never written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nofork
Run in the foreground instead of forking.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print every command and reply; implies
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BI \-p " port" "\fR, \fB\-\-socket\-port=" port
Listen for commands on this TCP port (default 5555).
.TP
.BI \-f " port" "\fR, \fB\-\-feedback\-port=" port
Send feedback on this TCP port (default none).
.TP
.BI \-s " dir" "\fR, \fB\-\-state\-root=" dir
The state root: the directory an LV2 plugin's
.B state:makePath
makes its files in, one directory
.BI lv2\- pid \- n
per instance, removed with it, and the copies a
.B state_save
makes of them,
.BI lv2\- pid \- n .saved ,
which stay. Default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/omx\-clap\-host ,
else
.IR ~/.local/state/omx\-clap\-host .
A root the host cannot make or write is said on stderr, and a plugin that
requires
.B state:makePath
is then refused with
.BR "resp \-102 hosting.features.missing" .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-follow\-jack\-transport
Hand every plugin the JACK transport: playing while it rolls, its frame as
the song position, its bars and beats when a timebase master gives them.
Without it the host's own transport, which
.BR set_bpm ", " set_bpb " and " transport
drive, is handed instead; the host never moves the JACK transport.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-channels =\fIchannels\fR
The lane's width, 1 or 2 (default 2). A plugin whose main ports differ is
asked for that many channels on each, mono or stereo, before it is
activated (clap.configurable\-audio\-ports, then clap.audio\-ports\-config);
one that cannot offer it keeps its declared layout, a JACK port per main
channel as always. A ports rescan the plugin reports asks again.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-idle\-hold =\fIoff\fR|\fImicroseconds\fR
What
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
is held at while a plugin is active (default 10: the cores wait in C1 between
cycles, a halt with a microsecond exit; 0 refuses every idle state, the cores
poll;
.B off
holds nothing). The library holds one descriptor for the process, opened at
the first active plugin and closed at the last, so a crash restores the
machine's default. A device that cannot be opened (its default mode is
root's; the package's udev rule gives the
.B audio
group write access) refuses nothing: the plugins run with no hold and the
refusal is said once on stderr and in every
.B idle_hold
answer, by code.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-show\-ui
Show every plugin's own window at its add, as
.B show_external_ui
does; the add answers as before whatever the window does.
.TP
.BR \-\-thread\-pool =\fIqualified\fR|\fIoff\fR|\fIon\fR
Which plugins run their clap.thread\-pool tasks on the process's helper
threads, one per core of the host's CPU affinity mask but the first, each
pinned to its core at the JACK thread's real\-time priority, the JACK thread
taking tasks too (docs/thread\-pool.md).
.B qualified
(the default) gives the pool to a plugin whose qualification measured it
real\-time safe and faster with it; the host is told of no such verdict on
its command line yet, so every plugin runs its tasks serial on the JACK
thread.
.B on
gives it to every plugin that asks: plugin\-hostd passes it to the worker of
a plugin whose pin grants the pool.
.B off
gives it to none. A pool that cannot run at the JACK thread's priority is
not made, and the plugin runs serial with one log line saying why; a wait
for a helper past the block is counted with the xruns.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-capabilities
Print what a planner may ask of this host, one word a line, and exit:
.B chain_expect
(a member joins a chain with no JACK client of its own) and
.B mono_in_stereo
(a member of one channel in and out runs inside a stereo chain, its input
the sum of both channels and its output given to both). A planner reads
this list, not the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH PROTOCOL
Commands are mod\-host's, one per message, terminated by a NUL byte, and
answered with
.B resp
.I code
using mod\-host's codes:
.BR add ", " remove ", " bypass ", " param_set ", " param_get ", " monitor_output ", " pin_expect ", " chain_expect ", "
.BR track_info ", " remote_pages ", " remote_page_get ", " param_info ", " chain ", " audio_ports ", "
.BR show_external_ui ", " hide_external_ui ", "
.BR preset_load ", " state_save ", " state_load ", " connect ", " disconnect ", " cpu_load ", " idle_hold ", " note_drops ", " note_out_drops ", "
.BR note_input ", " note_on ", " note_off ", " all_notes_off ", "
.BR set_bpm ", " set_bpb ", " transport ", " transport_sync ", " help ", " quit .
A plugin is named
.BI clap: path # plugin\-id
or
.BI lv2: uri ,
and a parameter by its decimal CLAP id, or an LV2 plugin's by its port symbol;
.B :bypass
reads and writes the bypass. A meter the plugin reports through
.B clap.meters/1
or
.B clap.gain\-adjustment\-metering/0
is an output symbol:
.B monitor_output
answers
.B "resp 1"
for one and
.B "resp 0"
otherwise, and the feedback port carries
.BI "output_set " "N symbol value"
whenever the value moves.
A value the plugin moves itself goes out on the feedback port as
.BI "param_set " "N param value" ,
between
.BI "param_gesture " "N param " begin
and
.BI "param_gesture " "N param " end
when its user holds the control, in the plugin's order, the last value of
each parameter per idle tick.
.BI "show_external_ui " N
puts the plugin's own window on the display, over clap.gui, trying in order:
Wayland floating, on the compositor
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
names, the plugin's own toplevel (CLAP has no Wayland parent); X11 embedded,
on the X display
.B DISPLAY
names, set into a window of the host's titled with the plugin's name; X11
floating. The first the plugin offers whose display answers is taken. Its
timers and descriptors run on the host's idle tick.
.BI "hide_external_ui " N
takes it off the display and keeps it;
.B remove
destroys it. When no window can be shown,
.B show_external_ui
answers
.B "resp \-404"
and a code, and the audio runs on:
.B hosting.window.no\-display
(neither
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
nor
.B DISPLAY
names a display that answers),
.B hosting.window.unsupported
(the plugin offers neither a Wayland nor an X11 window; a host built without
xcb shows Wayland alone) or
.B hosting.window.refused
(the plugin's own call failed).
.B idle_hold
answers
.BI "resp 0 " "target held aggregate active code" :
the CPU idle hold's target
.RB ( off
or microseconds), what the host holds now
.RB ( off
while no plugin is active or the device could not be opened), the device's
own answer (the kernel's limit across every holder, or
.B \-
when it cannot be read), the plugins active, and
.B hosting.idle.hold\-refused
or
.B \-
.
.BI "idle_hold " off | microseconds
changes the target at once, written to the open descriptor while held;
.B resp \-902
for a word that is neither.
.BI "note_drops " N
answers
.BI "resp 0 " "dropped releases sysex unmappable" :
the note events instance
.I N
had no room for since it was added, of them the releases (a note off, all
notes off, all sound off, a pedal let go), the sysex messages past a cycle's
eight, and the messages no dialect of the plugin carries. A cycle keeps the
last 128 of its 256 note slots for releases, so a flood of note ons never
crowds out the note off that ends one.
.BI "note_input " N
answers
.B "resp 0 midi_in"
for an instance that takes notes and
.B "resp 0 \-"
for one that takes none.
.BI "note_on " "N channel key velocity" ,
.BI "note_off " "N channel key velocity"
and
.BI "all_notes_off " N
play the instance as a MIDI note on, a note off, or all notes off on every
channel, at the start of its next cycle and ahead of what
.B midi_in
carries in it: channel 0 to 15, key 0 to 127, a note on's velocity 1 to 127
and a note off's 0 to 127, else
.BR "resp \-902" ;
.B "resp \-513"
for an instance that takes no notes.
.BI "note_out_drops " N
answers
.BI "resp 0 " "dropped unmapped port_full" :
the events instance
.I N
sent out that its ring of 256 had no room for, those with no MIDI form (a note
expression, a sysex, another note output's), and the messages
.B midi_out
had no room for, since it was added.
.BI "pin_expect " "N scheme" : sha256
is plugin\-hostd's: the next
.B add
of instance
.I N
compares the plugin's parameter layout in plugin\-hostd's
.B omx\-layout/1
serialisation with it after
.B init
and before
.BR activate ,
and when it differs destroys the instance and answers
.BR "resp \-510" ;
a scheme the host does not know answers
.BR "resp \-508" .
.BI "chain_expect " "N chain"
is plugin\-hostd's: the next
.B add
of instance
.I N
joins chain
.I chain
at its end, in the head's client, with no client of its own; that
.B add
answers
.B "resp \-902"
and leaves nothing when this host runs no such chain or the plugin is not as
wide as the head's output.
.BI "audio_ports " N
answers on one line
.BR "resp 0" ,
.B dry
while the instance passes dry, and a record per port registered at
.BR add ,
inputs first and each side's main port first, separated by spaces:
.IB side : role : index : channels : kind\fR[\fB:\fIlayout\fR][\fB:assumed\fR]\fB:"\fIname\fB":\fIjack\fB,\fIjack ,
the layout a surround port's CLAP surround ids or an ambisonic port's ordering
and normalisation joined by
.BR + ,
the name's
.B \(dq
and
.B \e
escaped with a backslash, as in
.BR "in:aux:1:1:mono:\(dqin 1\(dq:aux_in_1_1" .
.BI "track_info " "N name color" " [" kind ]
passes the strip's name, colour and kind to the plugin through
.BR clap.track\-info ;
.BI "remote_pages " N
and
.BI "remote_page_get " "N page"
read
.BR clap.remote\-controls ,
or, for a plugin without it, pages made of its
.B clap.params
(one per module path, eight parameters to a page),
and the feedback port carries
.BI "remote_pages_changed " N
when the plugin changes its pages,
and the host's own JACK xrun count as
.BI "xruns " count
(since its start, when it changes, at most once a second, none before the first xrun);
.BI "param_info " "N id"
answers
.BI "resp 0 " "unit scale min max default step symbol"
from the plugin's
.B org.openmixer.param\-contract/1
and its
.BR clap.params ,
and
.B "resp \-511"
for a plugin whose contract breaks a rule, which the log names. A plugin that
offers no contract at all is described by its
.B clap.params
alone (unit
.BR none ,
scale
.B stepped
for a stepped or enum parameter and
.B linear
otherwise, symbol
.BR param_ id ),
never by a mixture of the two.
.BI "chain " "chain N" " [" N ...]
is plugin\-hostd's: the instances, in this order, run in one JACK client, the
first one's, whose main input ports are the chain's input and main output
ports its output; every other member closes its client, so the chain is one
node of the graph. Each member is called in order inside that client's process
callback and runs as it does in a client of its own; bypass, parameters and
every other command still name one member by its number, and the first
member's ports report the members' latencies summed. A line for a chain
already running changes its members in place: a member it leaves out leaves
the walk and gets a client of its own back, the members it keeps take its
order in one step, so a move never runs a cycle without the moved member, and
a member it names anew joins at its place, one step each. A line that adds
members therefore passes, for a cycle or more each, through the new order
without the members still to join. Every member must be an instance this host
holds, named once, in no other chain, its main input and output as wide as the
first member's main output, and no member but the first may have a note port
or an auxiliary port (a side chain would read silence); else
.B "resp \-902"
.RB ( "resp \-3"
for an instance this host does not hold) and nothing changes. The clients the
members leaving go back to are made before anything changes, so a client JACK
cannot make refuses the line with nothing changed; a member that JACK will not
stop refuses it the same; a member whose client JACK will not activate stays
in the chain, at its end, and the line answers the error.
Every other mod\-host command answers
.BR "resp \-902" .
The README of the source distribution lists each command with its
arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB dir /effect_ N .clapstate
The state of instance
.I N
written by
.BI "state_save " dir
and read back by
.BR state_load ,
one blob per instance, beside mod\-host's
.BI effect\- N
directories when both hosts share
.IR dir .
.TP
.IB root /lv2\- pid \- n /
The files an LV2 plugin made through
.BR state:makePath ,
under the state root
.RB ( \-s );
removed with the instance.
.TP
.IB root /lv2\- pid \- n .saved/
The copies of them a
.B state_save
made, named by the saved state; they stay.
.TP
.I /tmp/omx\-clap\-host.pid
The pid of the forked host.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
The state root is
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/omx\-clap\-host
without
.BR \-s ;
an unset or relative value is
.IR ~/.local/state .
.TP
.B LV2_PATH
The directories searched for the bundle of an LV2 plugin, separated by
.BR : ;
unset, ~/.lv2, /usr/local/lib64/lv2, /usr/local/lib/lv2, /usr/lib64/lv2 and
/usr/lib/lv2.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
The compositor a plugin's own window goes on first, when the plugin offers a
Wayland window: a socket name in
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
or an absolute path.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display a plugin's own window goes on when no Wayland window is taken.
.TP
.B OMX_CLAP_HOST_CORE_DUMPS
A plugin that aborts or faults takes the host down with it. By default the
host then dies at once, of the same signal, without a core dump: writing the
core keeps every plugin of the host silent for 100 ms or more before its
supervisor can start it again. Set this to
.B 1
to keep the core dumps, for a crash to debug.
